{
  "$comment": "Which PKM branches an app feature owns because it needs them to work, and which writers may change them. One truth table read by the Python loader (consent-protocol/hushh_mcp/consent/reserved_branches.py) and the TypeScript one (hushh-webapp/lib/pkm/reserved-branches.ts). Hand-edited on purpose, changed only by a reviewed release. Byte-identical copies live in consent-protocol/contracts/pkm/ (the backend image is built from consent-protocol/) and hushh-webapp/contracts/pkm/ (the webapp imports it); parity tests fail on any drift.",
  "$comment_phase": "Phase 1 adds enforcement behind the `enforcement` switch below. In `shadow` the server and the device only count what they would refuse (pkm.reserved_would_refuse, labels only). In `enforce` the server refuses with 403 PKM_RESERVED_BRANCH_WRITER_FORBIDDEN (422 PKM_WRITER_UNKNOWN for an uncatalogued writer; for a client older than min_client_version the catalog still applies and only the client-dependent checks are skipped, with 409 PKM_RESERVED_REGISTRY_OUTDATED only when its writer is unknown) and the device returns blocked_reserved_branch. The switch lives here, not in an env flag, so every surface reads one reviewed value; the migration release (Phase 2) flipped it to `enforce` after the PKM upgrade gate moved agent-written entries to their siblings (the reserved_branch_migration_version manifest marker; no domain contract version changes, so builds already shipped keep writing). Rollback is this one value back to `shadow`: no data changes, the next deploy of the backend and web app reads it, and the migrated entries stay where the upgrade put them.",
  "$comment_enforcement": "`shadow` or `enforce`. Read by hushh_mcp/consent/reserved_branches.py enforcement_mode() and lib/pkm/reserved-branches.ts RESERVED_ENFORCEMENT_MODE. Any other value fails the loaders closed.",
  "enforcement": "enforce",
  "$comment_memory_screen_policy": "How the Memory screen treats items inside a reserved branch. `read_only_reserved`: the owning app screen edits and deletes them, and Memory shows `Open in <app>` from the entry's offer_action; items in agent_memory siblings stay editable. `editable`: Memory edits reserved items itself, as before Phase 1 (its writers pkm_memory_edited_button and pkm_memory_deleted_button are then refused in enforce mode, because no entry lists them). Reversible by changing this one value.",
  "memory_screen_policy": "read_only_reserved",
  "version": 1,
  "$comment_min_client_version": "The oldest client that understands this registry: the vault-write protocol level (hushh-webapp/lib/vault/write-protocol-version.ts PKM_CLIENT_VERSION), sent as mutation_plan.client_version on writes and as x-hushh-client-version on the PKM upgrade and metadata routes. Builds before the registry send neither. Unread in shadow mode.",
  "min_client_version": "2.0.0",

  "$comment_writers": "The closed writer catalog. A writer id is the normalized `source` of a PKM write authorization (hushh-webapp/lib/personal-knowledge-model/mutation-plan.ts normalizedWriterId), which reaches the server as mutation_plan.writer_id; the server derives `pkm_upgrade_orchestrator` for an upgrade-claim write and falls back to structure_decision.source_agent otherwise. Every label used in code must appear here (hushh-webapp/__tests__/lib/pkm/reserved-branches.test.ts fails otherwise). class: `feature` is an app feature's own control; `memory_agent` is the chat and capture pipeline, which may never write a reserved branch; `migration` is the PKM upgrade gate, whose authority is the server-verified upgrade claim, so it is never refused here. Writer identity is a governed catalog, not cryptography: the owner's client can always write its own vault, and the threat addressed is our own agent pipelines.",
